**PASS.** The game at localhost:8000 loads and starts with no defects. I watched it start from an empty browser cache, and this matches the previous site review on the same build.

**Build checked:** HEAD e500c44 plus the same uncommitted changes as the earlier reports (diff hash 92e25d72…), with `doom.wasm` hash 7afb94fa…. `dist/` was already up to date (`make -n` had nothing to do), so I didn't rebuild. Nothing was running on port 8000, so I started my own server. All 7 files came back 200 with the right content types and matched `dist/` byte for byte. I stopped that server at the end.

**What I saw in the browser (Playwright Chromium, 1280x720):**
- **Loading:** every file loaded with no failed requests and no page errors. The engine log shows the WAD loading and the game starting.
- **First image:** the title screen appeared about 116 ms after the page loaded, with correct colours and black bars on the sides.
- **Speed:** after startup the game ran at 35.0 tics/s, which is normal Doom speed. The longest gap between browser frames was 10.4 ms, so the page never froze.
- **Menu:** Escape opens and closes the main menu, and the demo keeps running behind it, as in the original game.
- **Layout:** the game keeps its 16:10 shape at both 1280x720 and 800x600, with no scrollbars.
- **Colour tints:** two screenshots have one. The red one is the game's damage flash and the yellow one is its item-pickup flash. Both are normal Doom effects, not colour bugs.
- **Console:** 0 errors and 2 warnings. Both say Chrome blocked audio until the user clicks, which is expected browser behaviour.

**Still open, carried over from earlier reports (none of them startup bugs):**
- **Coder:** the tab title briefly reads "Doom (WASM)" for about 110 ms before the game sets "DOOM Shareware". Cosmetic only.
- **Tester:** the speed checks need to allow for the roughly 1-second freeze during the screen-melt transition. The fault-injection script also lists the wrong checks for two faults: it claims one colour check (B6) catches the red/blue swap when it doesn't, and it leaves out G3b (door opened) for the disabled-use fault.
- **Not checked by anyone yet:** Firefox, Safari, high-resolution displays, a truly hidden tab, and listening to the audio.

All the pass criteria I used were proposed by agents, not approved by you. My first browser script failed partway and left an extra probe running, so each timing sample in `cold-load.json` appears twice. This doesn't change any result.

